<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scottish Highlands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Highlands of Scotland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scottish Highlands are the mountainous north and west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, beyond the Highland Boundary Fault. Home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> peoples recorded by Ptolemy and later to the Gaelic-speaking clans, they kept a distinct culture into modern times. Evictions during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/highland-clearances">Highland Clearances</a> of the 18th and 19th centuries sent many Highlanders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands.jpg" alt="Scottish Highlands" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jrockley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scottish_Highlands_and_Lowlands.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
